ost  from massive increases like that  well as you say these are globally  traded commodities uh the prices are  being felt everywhere in the world but  but clearly the the poorest are feeling  the brunt and and in particular those  those countries that depend heavily on  wheat for consumption so if you look at  countries of the north africa middle  east central asia those are countries  that that consume about twice that of  what we we consume in the u.s or in  europe uh in on a per capita basis so  it's a big part of the diets in egypt  it's 35 of the calories come from wheat  products like bread and uh there you've  seen you know unsubsidized wheat prices  or  bread prices rise astronomically uh  thankfully there are targeted subsidies  in egypt to try to address problems for  the the poorest households but for  countries that have very little  resources to provide towards consumers  and to help  mitigate some of these price impacts  they're the ones that are suffering the  most  now the kremlin denies that it is to  blame for the blockade of ukrainian  ports and the un and the african union  are pushing for them to be reopened how  optimistic should we be that we will see  that happen  i think it's very difficult i would  certainly hope that those ports would be  reopened  it it's difficult  to get those sorts of assurances that uh  i think the world's going to need i mean  remember that these aren't necessarily  countries  to owning the ships these are private  companies they they require  assurances that that their their ships  won't be targeted uh so insurance rates  are extremely high right now and right  now it's just physically uh they're  unable to to move into those ports  because of the mines and because of the  threats even before i mean remember that  russia actually was blockading those  ports a week before the the soldiers  actually came across borders so this has  been a very big problem and it's  expensive to move grain any other way uh  so going out through the west on rail or  barge is just four times the cost of  going down through the black sea okay  i'd like to come back to you in just a  moment but first let's hear from the  world food program uh they've been  warning of global famine if access to  black sea ports is not reopened  so the black sea ports are as it were  the silver bullet when it comes to  avoiding global famines global hunger  but if that silver bullet isn't found we  also need those alliances to support  overland deliveries of food um exporting  from this country a country that fed 400  million people last year  so they're calling those ports a silver  bullet um you touched on this just  briefly but do you think that overland  roots can be a realistic alternative  frankly in the short run i think it's  going to be very difficult i mean it is  you can do it and they are doing it uh  so but it's essentially means shipping  by rail  going upstream if you will because all  the grain typically now comes down  through those ports but you can go back  up through the the out through  into  the  river ports on the danube down to get  ports on in romania or up through poland  and in the baltics to try to get access  to the baltic sea but remember that the  that it adds a lot of cost to do so the  rail gauges aren't the same so that is  the the rail tracks are  are wider in ukraine than they are in in  um romania they have to be offloaded  reloaded  put onto barges then taken down canals  to get to the the blacks to the black  sea and all this adds an enormous amount  of cost plus it just can't handle the  volumes that we typically see going out  of the black sea uh in those ports  around odessa sounds like a huge  logistical challenge  longer term it's not going to be just  about supplies is it but also about  future harvests ukraine spring wheat  crop should be going into the ground  already for harvest this fall for  example how do you see that playing out  in the long term  yeah we we have a bigger problem in the  near term we have the fall planted wheat  uh that is coming uh that right now  people predict around 20 million tons uh  remember that we have about 20 million  tons already stuck in ukraine from last  year that hasn't been marketed which  typically would be marketed before the  new crop comes in and so i think that  that  the the challenge is going to be how to  store that without losing it because it  right now it just can't move in the  volumes that that typically would be  needed  and so um  that means putting it into storage  facilities some of which have been  damaged by the war but the bigger  problem is that they're already  i won't say completely full but they are  already far fuller than they should be  at this time of year so that means  storing grain on the ground or finding  temporary storage all of which adds cost  joe glauber from the international food  policy research institute thank you so  much for taking the time to speak with  us thanks claire
 

